Document (dev.stxt.website): RFCs and technical proposals

	Metadata: 
		Author: ChatGPT 5.2
		Last modif: 2026-08-22

	Header: RFCs and technical proposals

	Content >>
		RFCs (*Request for Comments*) and technical proposal documents serve to make
		design decisions in an explicit, reviewable, and shared way.

		They are usually long, argumentative, living documents that combine
		narrative text with structured information.

	Assert >>
		@STXT@ serves as a source format for this type of document without changing
		the usual workflow.

	Subheader: What is meant here by RFC

	Content >>
		In this context, “RFC” includes:

		* Internal technical proposals.
		* Classic RFCs, in the style of the IETF.
		* ADRs (*Architecture Decision Records*).
		* Design documents prior to implementation.

		No specific process is assumed: the subject is the document, not the
		methodology.

	Subheader: Common problems with current RFCs

	Content >>
		In practice, these documents tend to:

		* Live in Markdown with implicit conventions.
		* Mix metadata and free text without a clear separation.
		* Depend on the authors' discipline to maintain the structure.
		* Be difficult to validate automatically.

		The workflow itself, however, usually works:

		* Plain text.
		* Versioned in Git.
		* Reviewed by diff.

		@STXT@ keeps that workflow and adds explicit structure.

	Subheader: Example 1 — Minimal RFC

	Content >>
		A reduced RFC, sufficient for the initial discussion.

	Code >>
		RFC (com.acme.rfc):
			Id: RFC-012
			Title: Distributed cache for module X
			Status: Draft
			Authors:
				Author: Joan Costa
			Created: 2026-01-11

			Context >>
				Module X has latency problems under load.
				Currently each instance queries the database
				for repetitive operations.

			Proposal >>
				Introduce a shared distributed cache layer
				across instances to reduce database accesses.

			Decision >>
				Pending evaluation.

	Subheader: What @STXT@ brings here

	Content >>
		* Long text lives in text blocks (`>>`), without restrictions.
		* Metadata are nodes, not conventions.
		* The RFC structure is visible at a glance.

	Subheader: Example 2 — More complete RFC

	Content >>
		An RFC with alternatives, impact, and risks.

	Code >>
		RFC (com.acme.rfc):
			Id: RFC-013
			Title: Unification of the configuration system
			Status: Review
			Authors:
				Author: Platform Team
			Created: 2026-01-05
			Updated: 2026-01-10

			Context >>
				Currently there are multiple configuration formats
				(JSON, YAML, properties) with different rules.

			Problem >>
				The diversity of formats makes validation,
				automation, and support difficult.

			Proposal >>
				Adopt STXT as the single configuration format
				for internal services.

			Alternatives:
				Alternative:
					Title: Keep current formats
					Pros >>
						Zero initial cost.
					Cons >>
						Fragmentation remains.

				Alternative:
					Title: Standardize on YAML
					Pros >>
						Existing tools.
					Cons >>
						Ambiguity and complex validation.

			Impact >>
				Teams will need to gradually migrate their configurations.

			Risks >>
				Initial resistance to change.

			Decision >>
				Under architecture review.

	Subheader: Example 3 — Status evolution

	Content >>
		An RFC changes status over time.
		The status is explicit data, not a sentence in the text.

	Code >>
		RFC (com.acme.rfc):
			Id: RFC-013
			Title: Unification of the configuration system
			Status: Accepted
			Authors:
				Author: Platform Team
			Accepted date: 2026-01-18

			Context >>
				Multiple configuration formats with different rules.

			Proposal >>
				Adopt STXT as the single configuration format.

			Decision >>
				The progressive adoption of STXT is approved
				as the standard configuration format.

			Consequences >>
				* New services will use STXT.
				* Existing ones will migrate incrementally.

	Subheader: Example 4 — Relationship between RFCs

	Content >>
		It is common to reference other documents.
		In @STXT@ these relationships can be explicit nodes.

	Code >>
		RFC (com.acme.rfc):
			Id: RFC-020
			Title: Deprecation of the legacy system
			Status: Draft
			Authors:
				Author: Joan Costa

			Related:
				Reference: RFC-013
				Reference: RFC-007

			Context >>
				This RFC depends on the adoption of the new configuration system
				described in RFC-013.

			Proposal >>
				Retire the legacy system once the services have migrated.

	Subheader: Example 5 — Comments and review

	Content >>
		An RFC can accumulate comments without mixing them
		with the main text.

	Code >>
		RFC (com.acme.rfc):
			Id: RFC-021
			Title: New deployment pipeline
			Status: Review
			Authors:
				Author: Platform Team

			Context >>
				Today every team maintains its own pipeline.

			Proposal >>
				Unify CI/CD pipelines into a single definition.

			Comments:
				Comment:
					Author: Mery Adams
					Date: 2026-01-12
					Text >>
						I’m concerned about the impact on teams with their own tooling.

				Comment:
					Author: Keyla Brown
					Date: 2026-01-13
					Text >>
						Has the migration cost been evaluated?

	Subheader: Example 6 — Minimal template for RFCs

	Content >>
		A lightweight template (`@stxt.template`) guarantees that no RFC omits the
		mandatory elements, without fixing the rest of the content.

	Code >>
		Template (@stxt.template): com.acme.rfc
			Description >>
				RFC: Minimal structure for technical RFCs
			Structure >>
				RFC:
					Id: (1)
					Title: (1)
					Status: (1) ENUM [Draft, Review, Accepted, Rejected, Deprecated]
					Authors: (1)
						Author: (+)
					Created: (?) DATE
					Updated: (?) DATE
					Accepted date: (?) DATE
					Related: (?)
						Reference: (+)

					Context: (1) TEXT
					Problem: (?) TEXT
					Proposal: (1) TEXT
					Decision: (?) TEXT
					Consequences: (?) TEXT

					Alternatives: (?)
						Alternative: (*)
							Title: (1) @Title
							Pros: (?) TEXT
							Cons: (?) TEXT

					Impact: (?) TEXT
					Risks: (?) TEXT
					Comments: (?)
						Comment: (*)
							Author: (1) @Author
							Date: (1) DATE
							Text: (1) TEXT

	Subheader: Why this case fits especially well

	Content >>
		RFCs are already:

		* Plain text.
		* Versioned in Git.
		* Reviewed by people.

		@STXT@ adds:

		* Explicit structure.
		* Processable metadata.
		* Optional validation.
		* Less dependence on implicit conventions.

		The nature of the document does not change.

	Subheader: Summary

	Content >>
		Using @STXT@ for RFCs and technical proposals makes it possible to:

		* Write argued documents in free text.
		* Keep a visible, consistent structure.
		* Automate review and traceability.

		The document does not become a formal language or code.
